<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=green-revolution">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Farming and food</p><h1 class="enc-title">Green Revolution</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1960s–1970s</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Green Revolution was the spread of high-yielding varieties of wheat and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a>, together with chemical fertilisers, pesticides and irrigation, across much of the developing world from the 1960s. Building on plant breeding in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> and at the International Rice Research Institute in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a>, it sharply raised harvests in countries such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a> and helped avert feared famines. Critics point to its environmental costs and its uneven benefits for poorer farmers.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/green-revolution.jpg" alt="Green Revolution" width="320" height="226" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Hannah Ritchie · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Index-of-cereal-production-yield-and-land-use_World.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The term Green Revolution was first used by William S. Gaud, administrator of the American aid agency USAID, in a speech on 8 March 1968. He set it against the violent Red Revolution of the Soviets and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/white-revolution">White Revolution</a> of the Shah of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>. Its genesis in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> was a lengthy visit in 1940 by the American Vice President-elect Henry A. Wallace, who was appalled at the small <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maize">maize</a> yields. He persuaded the Rockefeller Foundation to fund an agricultural station to hybridise maize and wheat, and hired a young Iowa agronomist, Norman Borlaug, to lead it. Borlaug received the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nobel-peace-prize">Nobel Peace Prize</a> in 1970.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The basic approach was to breed high-yielding varieties of cereals, expand irrigation, modernise management, distribute hybridised seed and supply synthetic fertilisers and pesticides. Semi-dwarfing genes were bred in so that the plants could absorb more nitrogen without falling over before harvest. In 1960 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a>, with the Ford Foundation and the Rockefeller Foundation, established the International Rice Research Institute. A cross made there in 1962 produced the rice IR8 in 1966, nicknamed Miracle Rice.</p><p>In 1961 Borlaug was invited to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> by M. S. Swaminathan, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a> was chosen as the first site. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a> limed the acid soil of its cerrado region from the 1960s. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>'s own programme began separately from the American one, with state sponsorship of research and peasant knowledge. Attempts to carry the programmes to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> have generally been less successful.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Cereal production more than doubled in developing nations between 1961 and 1985, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>'s annual wheat production rose from 10 million tons in the 1960s to 73 million in 2006. Studies credit the revolution with averted hunger, raised incomes and lower infant mortality. The Consultative Group on International Agricultural Research was established on 19 May 1971 as a network of research centres.</p><p>Critics raise further concerns. The new crops need far more fertiliser and pesticide, so the energy used to grow them rose faster than the harvest. A 1989 estimate counted around a million pesticide poisonings a year, about 20,000 of them fatal. Opinions differ over its effect on wild biodiversity, and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a> the credit scheme behind IR8 came by 1980 to serve mainly rich landowners.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Green Revolution” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Green Revolution</b> of the late 1960s, using high-yielding wheat and rice, ended dependence on food aid.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-india">Republic of India · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>1940s–1960s</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>Mexico</dd></div><div><dt>Credited to</dt><dd>Norman Borlaug · Henry A. Wallace · <a href="/empire/mexico">Mexico</a> · <a href="/empire/united-states">United States</a></dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>The article's lead says the changes first emerged in developed countries in the early 20th century, while its history dates the genesis to Henry A. Wallace's visit to Mexico in 1940; the term itself was first used by William S. Gaud on 8 March 1968.</p><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Built on</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic-revolution">Agriculture<span>c. 10,000 BCE</span></a><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/haber-process">Haber process<span>1909</span></a></div><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=green-revolution">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
